body: "## What the finding says today\n\n```\ncode: legacy_ordering_cycle\nseverity: info\npolicy: \"Ordering cycles between exclusively terminal items are historical contradictions with no scheduling effect.\"\nmessage: \"terminal-only execution-order cycle across 7 item(s)\"\nsample: [pm-6hhn, pm-defw, pm-gyfn, pm-met4, pm-murz, pm-xtfo, pm-zetb]\nremediation: \"Leave as history debt, or clean up in a dedicated changelog-safe closed-item batch; never repair closed items ad hoc.\"\n```\n\nThe evidence is the membership of the strongly connected component. It does not name a single edge, so \"clean up in a batch\" gives the executor nothing to remove. pm-hnc9w7's acceptance criterion asks for \"the single lowest-evidence edge per cycle, with the choice recorded\", which requires knowing what the edges are.\n\n## Reading the actual rows\n\nTaking the seven-member cycle above, each member stores both of these:\n\n```\npm-zetb:  blocked_by: pm-6hhn                       <- legacy scalar\n          dependencies: pm-6hhn,blocks,\"2026-03-06...\"  <- structured row\n```\n\nThe scalar says 6hhn must precede zetb. The row says zetb must precede 6hhn. `blocks` and `blocked_by` are declared inverses, so assembly normalizes the pair into a two-node ordering cycle. The seven items form a chain of six such overlapping pairs, and the chain closes into one component.\n\nThe cycle is not between the milestones. It is between two contradicting statements about the same pair, both stored on the same holder.\n\n## The signature across the whole corpus\n\nScanning every item document for a scalar `blocked_by: <id>` together with a structured row `<same id>,blocks`:\n\n- 269 holders carry a scalar `blocked_by` pointing at a real item id\n- 26 of those also carry the contradicting `blocks` row\n\nThe 26 holders: pm-gyfn, pm-murz, pm-defw, pm-zetb, pm-xtfo, pm-met4, pm-0vnr, pm-06t, pm-3gi, pm-15o, pm-8z7, pm-8kxm, pm-c8dz, pm-cwp, pm-dgb, pm-l4o, pm-nj3, pm-s9hl, pm-1tyv, pm-yv2, pm-g6qd, pm-pmd, pm-si1, pm-kj4, pm-nkx, pm-ysgr.\n\nThey account for all eight reported cycles and all 49 affected items. The hubs are pm-2xl (five holders), pm-54d (five), pm-b1w (three). One scan, one signature, complete coverage.\n\n## The cause is a migration direction error, and it is not live\n\nThe structured rows carry `created_at: \"2026-03-06T22:55:5x\"` and author `maintainer-agent`, contemporaneous with the scalar. The migration from scalar blockers to structured dependency rows wrote the holder's row as `blocks` when the holder's own scalar meant `blocked_by` — the exact inverse — and left the scalar in place.\n\nVerified against current code in a sandbox tracker: `pm update <B> --blocked-by <A>` writes scalar `blocked_by: A` and the structured row `A,blocked_by`. The direction is correct today and the sandbox audit reports zero ordering cycles. The product defect is fixed; only the data written by the old path survives.\n\n## Therefore the repair is mechanical\n\nFor each of the 26 holders, remove the single row `{id: <target>, kind: blocks}`. The scalar already produces the correct edge, so ordering is preserved, the contradiction disappears, and no ordering information is lost. That is the \"single lowest-evidence edge per cycle\" pm-hnc9w7 asks for, identified by rule rather than by judgement: the row is the one that contradicts the older scalar, was written by a bulk migration, and cites no evidence.\n\n## Why it is blocked\n\nRemoving 26 `blocks` rows takes the graph from 12,105 to 12,079 edges against `graph-edges-floor: 12103` at `enforcement: block`. The repair is understood, safe and unexecutable until the floor is denominated over a population that does not count contradictions — which is pm-mfvsng."
